    <h1>Discovery</h1>
    <p>Research your product, market, and users before writing a single line of code. Discovery mode runs as a Head of Product with 15+ years of experience &mdash; it investigates, analyzes, and produces documentation artifacts. No code is written.</p>

    <h2>When to use</h2>
    <p>Run <code>/uc:discovery-mode {topic}</code> when you're starting a new product, exploring a new market, defining user personas, or researching competitors. It's the right first step when you don't yet know exactly what to build.</p>

    <h2>What happens</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Strategic scoping</strong> &mdash; parses your topic, defines focus areas, and decides which artifacts to produce</li>
      <li><strong>Parallel research</strong> &mdash; spawns two agents simultaneously: one explores your codebase and existing docs, the other researches competitors, market trends, and user archetypes</li>
      <li><strong>Synthesis</strong> &mdash; merges findings, separates content by perspective (product vs. research), flags decisions that need your input</li>
      <li><strong>Documentation</strong> &mdash; writes artifacts to <code>documentation/</code> following the canonical structure</li>
      <li><strong>Summary</strong> &mdash; top findings, artifact paths, open questions, and recommended next steps</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What it produces</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Product Description</strong> &mdash; vision, positioning, capabilities (always produced)</li>
      <li><strong>Research Report</strong> &mdash; competitors, market trends, evidence</li>
      <li><strong>Requirements</strong> &mdash; formal requirements with acceptance criteria</li>
      <li><strong>User Personas</strong> &mdash; evidence-based profiles with goals and pain points</li>
    </ul>

    <details>
      <summary>How parallel research works</summary>
      <div class="details-content">
        <p>Two research agents run simultaneously to maximize coverage:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Internal (Explore agent)</strong> &mdash; investigates your existing codebase, documentation, and external library references. Finds patterns, capabilities, and constraints that exist today.</li>
          <li><strong>External (Market Analyzer agent)</strong> &mdash; searches the web for competitor analysis, market trends, technology landscape, and user archetypes. Cites sources for every claim.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Results are merged in the synthesis phase with strict content separation &mdash; product descriptions contain user-facing information, research reports contain market data. No duplication across document types.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Content separation rules</summary>
      <div class="details-content">
        <p>Each document type has a distinct perspective. Content never duplicates across types:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Product description</strong> &mdash; how the platform works from the user's view</li>
          <li><strong>Research report</strong> &mdash; external market context, competitors, trends</li>
          <li><strong>Requirements</strong> &mdash; what problems to solve, acceptance criteria</li>
          <li><strong>Personas</strong> &mdash; who the users are, their goals and pain points</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Documents cross-reference each other rather than restating content.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="callout callout-purple">
      <p><strong>Challenge your findings.</strong> Run <code>/uc:critical-brainstorm</code> after discovery to stress-test your product decisions, surface hidden risks, and debate trade-offs before committing to a direction.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>After discovery, use <code>/uc:roadmap</code> to decompose your product into a sequenced series of plans, or jump directly to <a href="/docs/feature-planning">/uc:feature-mode</a> to plan your first feature.</p>

  </main>
</div>

